    {/* The research it scores against */}
    <section class="mb-12 md:mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-head text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink md:text-3xl">Why research backing matters</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        "Research-backed" is easy to assert and harder to demonstrate. The standard to
        hold a tool to is whether it names the work it relies on. GEO Optimizer's 47
        citability methods trace to two papers you can read: the Princeton GEO study
        (KDD 2024), which tested optimization methods at scale, and AutoGEO (arXiv:2510.11438, preprint),
        which reports an average improvement of about 36% over the Princeton baseline. The same body of
        work documents citation-lift signals such as +41% from adding quotations and +33%
        from adding statistics.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        You do not have to take those numbers as guarantees for your own site — model
        behavior varies — but a tool that points to specific, citable research is one you
        can interrogate. A tool that asserts "AI-optimized" with no source is not.
        For the underlying discipline, the
        {' '}
